&lt;p&gt;    &lt;a href=&quot;https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/IGNITE-1884&quot; title=&quot;.Net: JNI local ref can&amp;#39;t be accessed from another thread&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;IGNITE-1884&quot;&gt;&lt;del&gt;IGNITE-1884&lt;/del&gt;&lt;/a&gt; .Net: JNI local ref can&apos;t be accessed from another thread&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;hr /&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="14998948" author="ptupitsyn" created="Tue, 10 Nov 2015 17:22:05 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Fixed by creating temporary global references.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15000088" author="vozerov" created="Wed, 11 Nov 2015 07:59:25 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Pavel, I see several problems here:&lt;br/&gt;
1) SAFE_FUNC macro can throw an exception if callback handler is not installed =&amp;gt; global ref leak&lt;br/&gt;
2) Callback itself can throw an exception =&amp;gt; another leak&lt;br/&gt;
3) In ~50% cases store callback is null =&amp;gt; no need for global refs.&lt;br/&gt;
4) In most cases user will operate on references the same thread + some of affected callbacks could be on hot path (e.g. Cache.Load/LoadAll), so we need to minimize overhead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What if we introduce another UnmanagedHandle type - &quot;thread-bound handle&quot;? When handle is created we record current thread. Then on every access to handle we compare current thread with creator thread. If they differ - we fallback to &quot;global ref&quot;. In the end this global ref is cleaned up in callback finally block. &lt;br/&gt;
This should be both safe and performant. Thoughts?&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15000129" author="ptupitsyn" created="Wed, 11 Nov 2015 09:00:18 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;1,2) Both of these cases go down to ThrowToJava. It just sets an exception in Java, but does not affect C++ program flow. So the handle won&apos;t leak.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;a href=&quot;http://mindprod.com/jgloss/jni.html#EXCEPTIONS&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;http://mindprod.com/jgloss/jni.html#EXCEPTIONS&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, in future there may be the case with C++ or some other client where exceptions are possible. I&apos;ll address this with a wrapper/destructor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;3,4) Creating a wrapper and checking thread id would also introduce an overhead. Before doing this, we need to make sure if that is actually faster than GlobalRef. Let&apos;s do that separately.&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15002201" author="vozerov" created="Thu, 12 Nov 2015 14:59:33 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;I slightly changed implementation to account for failed attempt to create global reference (e.g. due to OOME).&lt;/p&gt;</comment>
                            <comment id="15010622" author="githubbot" created="Wed, 18 Nov 2015 09:42:05 +0000"  >&lt;p&gt;Github user ptupitsyn closed the pull request at:&lt;/p&gt;

